ions. Need provide interpretations. Could be from temporal logic perspective. Need identify plausible meanings. For each entry, write all interpretations plausible without consulting proposed readings. Need include timing and scope. We can produce multiple interpretations if ambiguous.\n\nNeed handle 88 entries. Let's enumerate and craft interpretations.\n\nGeneral: propositions map letters to events/properties. Requirement natural language. Need interpretations. Could include:\n- eventual occurrence: at least one occurrence somewhere.\n- keep occurring: infinitely often / repeatedly.\n- every request followed by pulse and flash: universal response, order, possibly same step, next step, eventually.\n- before any stop: precedence.\n- consecutive steps: both active simultaneously for two steps.\n- until release: hold from some point until release.\n- completion afterward: order.\n- immediately followed by: next step? Could be same? Usually immediately after = next step. Need mention.\n\nNeed maybe produce JSON entries. Need comments maybe \"Open interpretation only.\" Could be empty. But required. Use concise.\n\nLet's design interpretations. Need maybe not overdo but enough. Could have one interpretation per entry, but instructions say add slots if needed. For ambiguous, multiple. Need plausible. We can include 1-3.\n\nNeed ensure schema minItems 1. AdditionalProperties false. Comments string. review_id string.\n\nLet's go entry by entry.\n\nR001: \"A pulse and a flash must eventually occur.\" props a pulse, b flash. Interpretations:\n1. At some step in future, a pulse occurs and at some (possibly same or different) step a flash occurs.\n2. There is a finite time by which both a pulse and a flash have occurred, not necessarily same step or order.\nMaybe distinct: simultaneous eventual occurrence? \"eventually occur\" could mean both eventually, maybe same step? Could include \"both occur at some point; order unrestricted\". Comments.\n\nR002: \"Pulses and flashes must keep occurring.\" props a pulse, b flash. Interpretations:\n1. Infinitely often both pulses and flashes occur (each kind infinitely often).\n2. From some point onward, pulses and flashes continue to occur repeatedly, perhaps not necessarily alternating.\nNeed include timing.\n\nR003: \"Every request must be followed by a pulse and a flash.\" props a pulse, b flash, c request. Interpretations:\n1. For every step with request, at some later step(s) both pulse and flash occur; order unspecified.\n2. For every request, a pulse and flash occur after it, possibly same step or immediately next, with both required.\nNeed scope universal.\n\nR004: \"A pulse and a flash must occur before any stop.\" props a,b,c stop. Interpretations:\n1. If a stop ever occurs, there must be at least one pulse and one flash at earlier steps than that stop.\n2. The first stop, if it occurs, is preceded by both pulse and flash; if no stop, requirement vacuously? But wording must occur before any stop maybe if stop occurs. Could include unconditional? \"must occur before any stop\" maybe they must occur and no stop until after both. Interpretations: (a) no stop can occur until both have occurred; (b) if stop occurs, both occurred earlier. Need maybe both.\n\nR005: \"After each request, a pulse and a flash are required before the next stop.\" props a,b,c request,d stop. Interpretations:\n1. For every request, before the next stop after it, both pulse and flash must occur.\n2. If no next stop occurs, the pulse and flash must still occur eventually? Wording required before next stop implies if stop occurs; but could require response even absent stop. Need include.\n\nR006: \"The signal and the indicator must eventually be active for two consecutive steps.\" props a,b. Interpretations:\n1. There exists a step t such that both signal and indicator are active at t and t+1.\n2. Both must be active simultaneously for a run of at least two consecutive steps at some point.\n\nR007: \"The signal and the indicator must eventually become active.\" props a,b. Interpretations:\n1. At some future step, signal active and at some future step indicator active, not necessarily same step.\n2. Both eventually become active, possibly simultaneously, without duration requirement.\n\nR008: \"Eventually the signal and the indicator must be active until a release occurs.\" props a,b,c release. Interpretations:\n1. From some step onward, both active continuously until a release step; release may or may not be required? \"until a release occurs\" could imply release eventually occurs? Need include: until release, if release occurs; if no release, active forever.\n2. There is a point at which both become active and remain active through all steps before the first subsequent release.\n\nR009: \"A pulse and a flash must occur with a completion afterward.\" props a,b,c completion. Interpretations:\n1. There exists a pulse and a flash (order unspecified) before some completion event.\n2. At some point, a pulse and flash occur and later a completion occurs.\n\nR010: \"After a start, a pulse and a flash must occur before a finish.\" props a,b,c start,d finish. Interpretations:\n1. If a start occurs, then before the next finish after it, both pulse and flash must occur.\n2. The first finish after a start is preceded by pulse and flash; if no finish, maybe still eventually? Need include.\n\nR011: \"A pulse immediately followed by a flash and a click immediately followed by a mark must eventually occur.\" props a,b,c,d mark. Interpretations:\n1. There is a step t where pulse occurs, at t+1 flash occurs; and separately a step u where click occurs, at u+1 mark appears.\n2. The two immediate pairs may occur at different times; \"immediately followed\" means next step.\n\nR012: \"The signal and the indicator must keep being active for two consecutive steps.\" props a,b. Interpretations:\n1. Infinitely often, there are two consecutive steps in which both are active.\n2. From some point onward, after any two-step window? Hmm \"keep being active for two consecutive steps\" could mean they remain active and every occurrence lasts at least two steps? Need likely: repeatedly they must be active for runs of at least two consecutive steps. Could also mean once active, they must stay active for at least two consecutive steps? But \"keep being active\" suggests infinitely often. Include two.\n\nR013: \"Every pulse must be followed by a flash.\" props a,b. Interpretations:\n1. For every pulse at step t, there is a flash at some later step.\n2. For every pulse, a flash occurs immediately/soon after? Need scope. Could include \"not necessarily same step\".\n\nR014: \"If there is a pulse now, a flash must follow.\" props a,b. Interpretations:\n1. At current step, if pulse occurs, then at some future step flash occurs.\n2. If pulse occurs now, flash occurs immediately next step? \"must follow\" could be later. Need include both.\n\nR015: \"There must be at least one pulse followed by a flash.\" props a,b. Interpretations:\n1. There exists a pulse at step t and a flash at some later step.\n2. At least one pulse-flash sequence occurs; other pulses unconstrained.\n\nR016: \"After every pulse, the indicator must then stay on permanently.\" props a pulse, b indicator on. Interpretations:\n1. For every pulse at t, from t (or t+1?) onward indicator remains on forever.\n2. Each pulse triggers permanent on state, possibly including pulse step. Need timing: \"then stay on\" maybe after pulse, from next step onward. Include.\n\nR017: \"A stop must occur, and before the first stop every pulse must be followed by a flash, even if that flash comes after the stop.\" props a,b,c stop. Interpretations:\n1. At least one stop occurs; for every pulse before first stop, there is some flash later (could be after stop).\n2. The obligation applies to pulses in prefix before first stop, but flash fulfillment can occur after stop.\n\nR018: \"Every pulse must be followed by a flash, with a chime at 